{
  "dom_id": "step-q87594",
  "name": "Question 14",
  "problem": "Solve the inequality $2\\cdot {5}^{3-x}+4>0.$",
  "worked_solution": "First, we isolate the exponential term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot {5}^{3-x}+4 & >0 \\\\ 2\\cdot {5}^{3-x} & >-4 \\\\ {5}^{3-x} & >-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, recall that a positive number raised to any real power is always positive. Since $5>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n{5}^{3-x}>0\n$$\n\nfor every real value of $x.$\n\nIn particular, every value of ${5}^{3-x}$ is greater than $-2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{5}^{3-x}>-2\n$$\n\nfor all real values of $x.$\n\nHence, the original inequality is satisfied for all real numbers.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
